      "writtenOn": "2026-04-02T12:05:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, Chrome, PDFium, V8, Dawn, and WebRTC all store `partition_alloc` in different paths. I recently updated the G3 config for Dawn to handle this (`go/shortlink-dawn-partition-alloc`) when porting partition_alloc to dawn.\n\nSince you mentioned nobody is using the Skia GN config, let\u0027s aim for a zero-impact solution, I guess I can\u0027t rely on embedders to depend on this config. What about adding an internal frontend header in `src/partition_alloc/pointers/raw_ptr.h`? Depending on a DEFINE, it redirects to either the real `partition_alloc` or our `raw_ptr_noop`.\n\nWe could put the 3 no-op files under a private subdirectory, like `src/partition_alloc/noop/pointers/raw_ptr.h`.\n\nThis way:\n\n* **Regular behavior:** No flags needed, internal code uses our no-op implementation.\n* **Behavior with partition_alloc (Chrome/Skia):** Requires 2 compiler flags: the DEFINE and the specific include directory.\n\nFor example, the frontend header `src/partition_alloc/pointers/raw_ptr.h` would  be:\n\n```cpp\n#if defined(SK_USE_PARTITION_ALLOC)\n    #include \"partition_alloc/pointers/raw_ptr.h\"\n#else\n    #include \"src/partition_alloc/pointers/noop/raw_ptr.h\"\n#endif\n```\n\nThis is different from what we did in Dawn, but that\u0027s a possibility. WDYT?",
      "parentUuid": "5e1f395f_db68e63b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 26,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 26,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "0b1dcb44d7e797b8569684000a224c432c91eac1",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
